#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# no-boilerplate - Agora - invariant: no starter kit boilerplate in what we publish.
#
# Origin (I-024): the `README.md` inherited from the Drupal CMS Site Template
# Starter Kit was PUBLISHED promising "a gorgeous theme that meets WCAG AAA
# standards" and describing a template "designed for professional electricians",
# with a broken link to `GET-STARTED.md`. It survived because no task owned the
# WHOLE file - T-112 owned one section of it - so the gate never lied, it was
# simply never asked. The same overpromise was also alive in the `description`
# fields of `composer.json` and `recipe.yml`, which are the strings the Drupal CMS
# installer puts in front of the user.
#
# Rider of [andres] 2026-08-21: this invariant runs after "a full initial sweep
# over config, demo content and descriptions - not just the README". That sweep
# was executed for T-309; its results are reported below in DELIBERATELY NOT
# DENY-LISTED.
#
# SCOPE - everything that gets published, in two passes:
#   (1) the packaged tree at HEAD: literally `git archive HEAD`, which is what
#       Drupal.org ships to end users;
#   (2) the working-tree copies of those same paths, so a reintroduction is
#       caught BEFORE it is committed.
# The process layer (specs/, .claude/, CLAUDE.md, tests/) is `export-ignore`d
# (D-015.2) and is therefore out of scope by construction, not by exclusion.
# That matters: `drupal_cms_site_template_base` appears legitimately in prose
# inside `specs/` and `IDIOMS.md` (and, since 2026-09-26, as a dependency in the
# packaged tree too - see the eighth term below). If this script ever reports a hit there, the
# SCOPE is wrong and must be fixed - never add an exclusion for it.
#
# Contract:
#   - prints both scopes, the files scanned, the deny-list size and the findings
#   - every finding is reported as file:line with the offending line
#   - exit 0 ONLY if findings = 0
#   - a scope that cannot be read (no git, no HEAD) is a FAILURE, never a skip
#     (I-007: an exit 0 without counts proves nothing).
#   - every packaged path is ACCOUNTED FOR in both passes, and a shortfall is
#     a FATAL rather than a finding (see the block immediately below).
#   - every skip is NAMED. A skip count nobody can attribute to a path is
#     refused, because that is the shape the defect below wore.
#
# ----------------------------------- partial scans: FATAL at zero was not --
# ----------------------------------- the same guard as FATAL at half ------
# T-0632, 2026-09-21. This script was FATAL at zero and SILENT AT PARTIAL.
# The I-028 guard further down refuses `scanned: 0`; nothing refused
# `scanned: half`, and printing two numbers is not comparing them.
#
# Measured on this package, twice, by two people:
#
#                     complete run     truncated run
#   scanned                    728               364
#   skipped as binary            6               370
#   findings                     0                 0
#   exit                         0                 0
#
# 370 - 6 = 364, which is exactly the set of files never searched. When
# pass 1 ended early the recount's `[ -f "$_tmp" ]` test found no HEAD copy
# for those paths and took its `else` branch, which incremented BINARY. **A
# path whose content was never produced is not a binary blob**, and calling
# it one turns "these files were not examined" into "these files were
# examined and are fine". Nothing in the summary said a pass ended early.
#
# TWO HONEST LIMITS ON THAT MEASUREMENT, so this block is not read as more
# than it is. The truncation was INDUCED, by wrapping the script in
# `timeout 120`, and NOTHING wraps it in `timeout` in CI - so it is not an
# observed CI defect and must not be described as one. What IS general is
# the question it exposed: a child dying mid-scan for any reason - OOM, a
# runner evicting a job, a killed pipe - was reported green.
#
# WHAT CLOSES IT, and none of it is pinned to a number:
#   (a) each pass writes a RECEIPT line per path it finishes, in the subshell
#       where the work actually happens. A receipt shorter than the declared
#       scope is a pass that ended early, and that is a FATAL.
#   (b) a packaged path with no HEAD copy in the work dir is counted as NOT
#       EXTRACTED - its own classification, never `binary`. Non-zero is a
#       FATAL. This is a SECOND, INDEPENDENT witness of (a): the two are
#       derived from different things - a receipt file written by the
#       scanning loop, and the work dir that loop populated - so a change
#       defeating one still trips the other.
#   (c) both passes must ACCOUNT for the whole declared scope:
#           pass 1:  text + binary + not-extracted  ==  scope
#           pass 2:  text + binary + absent         ==  scope
#           total :  scanned + skips                ==  scope x 2
#       The scope is `git archive HEAD` filtered to files - the same single
#       derivation both passes iterate - so a shortfall can only mean a pass
#       did less than it was asked to.
#   (d) every skip is NAMED in a ledger and the ledger's length is compared
#       against the counters. This one is about ATTRIBUTION rather than
#       independence, and it is written down that way: the ledger line and
#       the counter are incremented side by side. What it buys is that `6`
#       is never a bare number - a reader sees WHICH three paths, twice -
#       and that an emptied ledger cannot sit under a healthy count (I-028).
#
# THE ARITHMETIC ALONE WOULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT IT, and that is the part worth
# reading twice before anybody simplifies this. Measured on the induced
# truncation described above, with both passes stopped at 20 of 367:
# `accounted 734 of 734` and `skips named 350 of 350` BOTH HOLD. Every one of
# the four identities in (c) and (d) balances, because a path moved from
# `scanned` to a skip bucket is still accounted for - it is the CLASSIFICATION
# that is false, not the sum. A reconciliation that only added the totals up
# would have printed a clean-looking balance over a scan of 40 path-scans out
# of 734. What fires is (a) and (b): the receipts, and the bucket that says
# NOT EXTRACTED instead of `binary`.
#
# The same run shows why (a) is not redundant with (b). Pass 2's recount
# re-probes the LIVE working tree, which is there whether pass 2 read it or
# not - so a truncated pass 2 is invisible to every counter in this script
# except its receipt: the run above prints `pass 2 finished 20` beside
# `text 364`. Before T-0632 a pass 2 that stopped after one file reported 364
# worktree files scanned and exited 0.
#
# DO NOT replace any of this with a constant. `6` is true of this package on
# this day and of nothing else; binaries legitimately arrive and leave. Every
# figure above is derived from the archive listing at run time.
#
#
# House rules for tests/bin/ (T-321(c)): `wc -l` never `grep -c` for a compared
# value; numeric guards defaulted ONLY where zero means FAIL; grep rc >= 2 is an
# error, never "no match"; never `-F` with `-i`. The CANONICAL copy of the block,
# with the reasoning and the I-031 asymmetry spelled out, lives in the header of
# tests/bin/no-ci-allow-dev. Read it before adding a counter here.
#
# Usage: tests/bin/no-boilerplate   (run from anywhere; it cd's to the repo root)

set -u
# No `set -e`: a finding must be recorded and reported, not abort the run.

SCRIPT_DIR=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)
REPO_ROOT=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$SCRIPT_DIR/../.." && pwd)
cd "$REPO_ROOT" || { echo "FATAL: cannot cd to repo root $REPO_ROOT"; exit 1; }

C_OK=""; C_BAD=""; C_OFF=""; C_DIM=""
if [ -t 1 ]; then
  C_OK=$'\033[32m'; C_BAD=$'\033[31m'; C_OFF=$'\033[0m'; C_DIM=$'\033[2m'
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- deny-list --
# Verbatim strings from the starter kit, matched case-insensitively as BREs
# (see the scanner: -F is banned here, I-027). Each one is a phrase that
# describes a DIFFERENT product than Agora, or a placeholder the kit expects you
# to replace.
#
# FORBIDDEN: deleting a term from this list to turn the invariant green. A term
# leaves this list only when the string it guards can no longer be reintroduced,
# and that reasoning is recorded in specs/000-project/DECISIONS.md. Turning a
# gate green by shrinking its input is the failure mode this file exists to stop.
DENY_TERMS=$(cat <<'EOF'
electrician
WCAG AAA
GET-STARTED
MY_SITE_TEMPLATE_NAME
my-site-template
Site Template Starter Kit
my amazing site template
"name": "drupal/drupal_cms_site_template_base"
drupal-site-template
EOF
)
# ------------------------------------ the eighth term, narrowed (2026-09-26) --
# Until 2026-09-26 this term was the bare project name, which could only ever
# mean a leftover of the starter kit. Drupal CMS 2.2.0 made the same drupal.org
# project the home of "Drupal CMS Basics", the recipe that replaced the six
# retired base recipes, and this package now REQUIRES it (D-018, amended
# 2026-09-26). The bare name is therefore correct content in composer.json,
# recipe.yml, README.md and LICENCE-MANIFEST.md, and matching it would turn
# this invariant red on the truth: pipeline 977862 read 32 such findings.
# The term is NARROWED, not deleted, per the FORBIDDEN clause above: it now
# matches the kit's name only where the kit left it, as this package's OWN
# name in composer.json. The list stays at nine terms.
# --------------------------------------------------- the ninth term (T-703) --
# NEW COVERAGE, not a revert. The wave-1 rider's "adjusted check" was never in
# this file: it was the `.extra["drupal-site-template"]` tripwire in
# tests/bin/gate-a-wave1.sh, and the atomic swap (9dc5722) flipped it from
# 'present' to 'absent' in the same commit, exactly as that check's own header
# demanded. That half of T-703 was already discharged when T-703 ran. This term
# is the half that was NOT: the key had one watcher on the packaged tree, and
# now it has two facing in opposite directions - gate-a-wave1.sh fails if the
# JSON key COMES BACK, this list fails if the STRING reappears anywhere at all,
# in any packaged file, including the prose that describes the site to the user.
#
# WHY `drupal-site-template` AND NOT `extra\.drupal-site-template`, measured
# rather than argued (both run over `git archive 9dc5722~1`):
#   `extra\.drupal-site-template` -> 2 hits, BOTH in recipe.yml comments, and
#       NONE in composer.json. The key in the JSON is nested (`"extra": {`
#       ... `"drupal-site-template": {`), so the dotted path never appears as
#       a literal string in the file that actually carried the block. It would
#       have passed the row's criterion while missing the row's subject.
#   `drupal-site-template`        -> 3 hits, and the first is composer.json:28,
#       the block itself. 0 hits at HEAD, so the invariant stays green.
# No metacharacter, so no escaping is needed and the preflight above compiles it.
#
# WHY `blank` IS NOT HERE, also measured. The pre-audit's argument was sound -
# the README's `blank` claims had no watcher - but the term over-matches on
# CURRENT, CORRECT content. Over `git archive HEAD` it produces 4 hits and 0
# true positives: `.gitattributes:45` ("blanket"), README.md:128 and :136 (the
# prose that correctly describes THIS swap, naming the theme it replaced), and
# content/canvas_page/...yml:1 ("a totally blank Canvas landing page", which is
# the Canvas page, not the theme). `blank theme` narrows it to 2 hits and both
# are still the legitimate README prose. Adding any of them would turn this
# invariant red on the truth, and the only way back to green would be deleting a
# term - the exact move the FORBIDDEN clause above exists to stop. The README's
# `blank` claims are covered instead by T-705 re-measuring them on a rebuilt rig.

# T-321(a), house rule 1: `wc -l`, never `grep -c`. This value feeds the
# TERM_COUNT == 0 guard further down, and `grep -c` prints NOTHING on rc >= 2 -
# a blank would make `[ "" -eq 0 ]` exit 2, skip the branch, and walk straight
# past the very guard T-320(a) added (I-031). `wc -l` cannot emit a non-number.
TERM_MATCHED=$(printf '%s\n' "$DENY_TERMS" | grep -ve '^$')
TERM_RC=$?
if [ "$TERM_RC" -eq 0 ]; then
  TERM_COUNT=$(printf '%s\n' "$TERM_MATCHED" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
else
  TERM_COUNT=0
fi

FINDINGS=0
SCANNED=0
BINARY=0
MISSING=0

printf '=========================================================================================================\n'
printf 'no-boilerplate - Agora - starter kit strings must not reach what we publish (I-024)\n'
printf 'repo: %s\n' "$REPO_ROOT"
printf '=========================================================================================================\n'

# ------------------------------------------------------- grep's third state --
# T-316 / I-027. `grep` has THREE exit states, not two: 0 = matched, 1 = no
# match, >= 2 = grep itself FAILED (invalid pattern, unreadable file, crash).
# This script inferred "no hit" from "no stdout", so a grep that could not run
# read exactly like a clean file - a scanner that cannot scan reporting that
# everything is fine. That is how an invalid eighth deny term could be counted
# in `deny-list terms: 8` while scanning with seven.
#
# The scanning greps run inside `printf ... | while ... done >> "$HITS_FILE"`,
# i.e. in a subshell whose stdout IS the findings file: an `exit` there would
# not stop this script, and a message printed there would be counted as a
# finding. So a failure is recorded in a marker file and announced on stderr,
# and assert_grep_ok() below turns it into a loud, non-zero abort before a
# single count is printed. Never a warning, never a finding: "the file was not
# searched" is not "the file is clean". GREP_ERR_FILE itself is created further
# down, together with HITS_FILE, so that the existing trap owns its cleanup;
# neither function below runs before that.
grep_failed() { # <rc> <pattern> <subject>
  printf 'grep exit %s on %s while matching: %s\n' "$1" "$3" "$2" >> "$GREP_ERR_FILE"
  printf 'FATAL: grep failed (exit %s) on %s while matching: %s\n' "$1" "$3" "$2" >&2
}

assert_grep_ok() {
  [ -s "$GREP_ERR_FILE" ] || return 0
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s the scan did not complete - grep failed:\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF"
  sed 's/^/  /' "$GREP_ERR_FILE"
  printf 'A file that could not be searched is NOT a file with no boilerplate in it.\n'
  printf 'This run has NO result, which is not a clean result. (T-316 / I-027)\n'
  exit 1
}

# --------------------------------------------------- deny-list preflight ------
# Every deny term is used as a BRE (see the scanner). An INVALID one - an
# unmatched `[`, a trailing `\` - does not over-match: grep refuses to compile
# it, exits 2 and prints nothing, which is indistinguishable from "no match"
# unless the status is read. The whole list is therefore compiled once, HERE, in
# the parent shell where an abort actually stops the run, against empty input so
# that only the pattern is under test.
BAD_TERMS=0
while IFS= read -r _t; do
  [ -n "$_t" ] || continue
  printf '' | grep -Iin -- "$_t" >/dev/null 2>&1
  _trc=$?
  if [ "$_trc" -ge 2 ]; then
    printf '\n%sFATAL:%s deny-list term is not a pattern grep can compile (exit %s): [%s]\n' \
      "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$_trc" "$_t"
    BAD_TERMS=$((BAD_TERMS + 1))
  fi
done <<DENYCHECK
$DENY_TERMS
DENYCHECK
if [ "$BAD_TERMS" -gt 0 ]; then
  printf 'A term grep cannot compile scans NOTHING, while still being counted in\n'
  printf 'the deny-list size, so the run looks clean for a list it never actually applied.\n'
  printf 'Escape the metacharacter or remove the term. (T-316 / I-027)\n'
  exit 1
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------- resolve the scope ---
if ! git rev-parse --verify HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s no git HEAD - the packaged scope cannot be resolved.\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF"
  printf 'A scope that cannot be read is a FAILURE, never a skip.\n'
  exit 1
fi

ARCHIVE_LIST=$(git archive --format=tar HEAD 2>/dev/null | tar -tf - 2>/dev/null)
ARCHIVE_RC=$?
if [ "$ARCHIVE_RC" -ne 0 ] || [ -z "$ARCHIVE_LIST" ]; then
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s `git archive HEAD` produced nothing (exit %s).\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$ARCHIVE_RC"
  printf 'An empty archive would pass every "does not contain" check through the back door.\n'
  exit 1
fi

ENTRIES=$(printf '%s\n' "$ARCHIVE_LIST" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
# Directory entries end in `/` and have no content to scan; files are the scope.
PACKAGED_FILES=$(printf '%s\n' "$ARCHIVE_LIST" | grep -v '/$')
_dir_rc=$?
if [ "$_dir_rc" -ge 2 ]; then
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s the directory filter over the archive listing failed (grep exit %s).\n' \
    "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$_dir_rc"
  printf 'An empty scope would pass every "does not contain" check by construction.\n'
  exit 1
fi

# T-0632: the T-321(a) EXEMPTION that stood here is GONE, and saying so is the
# point of this comment rather than deleting it quietly. It read: "this is the
# one `grep -c` left in this file, and it is DISPLAY-ONLY - FILE_COUNT feeds
# the scope line below and nothing else. It never reaches a `[` test". That
# was TRUE when it was written. The reconciliation block now COMPARES this
# value, so the exemption's own premise has expired and the house rule applies
# again in full: `wc -l` never `grep -c` for a compared value, because
# `grep -c` prints NOTHING on rc >= 2 and a blank makes `[ "" -eq 0 ]` exit 2 -
# stepping OVER the guard rather than tripping it (I-031).
#
# SCOPE_FILES is also the ONE derivation of the scope from here on. Both
# passes and the recount read it, so they cannot iterate different sets - and
# the reconciliation below would be worth nothing if they could.
SCOPE_FILES=$(printf '%s\n' "$PACKAGED_FILES" | grep -ve '^$')
_scope_rc=$?
if [ "$_scope_rc" -ge 2 ]; then
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s the blank-line filter over the packaged file list failed (grep exit %s).\n' \
    "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$_scope_rc"
  exit 1
fi
if [ -n "$SCOPE_FILES" ]; then
  FILE_COUNT=$(printf '%s\n' "$SCOPE_FILES" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
else
  FILE_COUNT=0
fi

# I-007 / I-028. This is EARLIER than the SCANNED == 0 guard further down and
# does not replace it: the two answer different questions ("the scope is
# empty" against "the scope held nothing scannable"), and catching the first
# here saves spending ~6600 greps proving that nothing contains anything.
# Zero means FAIL, so `:-0` trips the guard instead of letting a blank step
# over it (T-321(b)).
if [ "${FILE_COUNT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s the packaged scope resolved to 0 files.\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF"
  printf 'A scan of nothing is not a clean result (I-007).\n'
  exit 1
fi

# The HEAD copy of each path is written to WORK_DIR under a name mangled with
# `tr '/' '_'`, which is NOT injective: `a/b` and `a_b` mangle to the same
# name. A collision lets one path's temp file be overwritten by another's;
# BOTH then look extracted, the recount reports two scans, and one path's HEAD
# content was never searched - a silent skip of exactly the family the
# reconciliation below exists to refuse, and one it could not see, because
# the file it looks for IS there. Green and non-vacuous today rather than
# decorative: 367 paths, 367 distinct names, measured before it was written.
MANGLED_COUNT=$(printf '%s\n' "$SCOPE_FILES" | tr '/' '_' | sort -u | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
if [ "${MANGLED_COUNT:-0}" -ne "$FILE_COUNT" ]; then
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s %s packaged path(s) collapse to %s distinct work-dir name(s).\n' \
    "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$FILE_COUNT" "$MANGLED_COUNT"
  printf 'Two paths sharing one temp file means one of them is never searched\n'
  printf 'while both are counted as scanned. Widen the mangling. (T-0632)\n'
  exit 1
fi

printf '\nscope resolved from `git archive HEAD` (%s):\n' "$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)"
printf '  %s%d tarball entries - %d files, %d directory entries%s\n' \
  "$C_DIM" "$ENTRIES" "$FILE_COUNT" "$((ENTRIES - FILE_COUNT))" "$C_OFF"
printf '%s\n' "$PACKAGED_FILES" | sed 's/^/    /'

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- scanner ---
# scan_one <display-label> <file-with-content>
# Reports every deny-list hit as `label:line: term` plus the offending line.
scan_one() {
  _label=$1; _file=$2
  grep -Iq . "$_file" 2>/dev/null
  _brc=$?
  # rc 1 = binary or empty, which is a real answer and is counted as such.
  # rc >= 2 was counted as "binary" too: a file silently dropped from the scan.
  [ "$_brc" -ge 2 ] && grep_failed "$_brc" "<non-empty-line test: the text/binary probe>" "$_file"
  if [ "$_brc" -ne 0 ]; then
    BINARY=$((BINARY + 1))
    return
  fi
  SCANNED=$((SCANNED + 1))
  printf '%s\n' "$DENY_TERMS" | grep -ve '^$' | while IFS= read -r _term; do
    # No `-F` here (I-027): GNU grep 3.0, which ships with Git for Windows/MSYS2,
    # SIGABRTs whenever `-F` and `-i` are combined - bisected on this host as
    # `-Fin`/`-IFin` -> rc=134, `-Iin` -> rc=0 with the correct hit. `-i` itself
    # is not optional: the deny list is matched case-insensitively by design
    # ("WCAG AAA" must also catch "wcag aaa"). Consequence: terms are now BRE,
    # not fixed strings, and a deny term carrying a regex metacharacter falls
    # into one of TWO cases. T-315 recorded only the first and called it "the
    # safe direction"; that claim was true of the first case and false of the
    # second, which is the one that bites (T-316):
    #
    #   VALID BRE - `.`, `*`, `\{2\}`: the term over-matches instead of matching
    #   literally. A false POSITIVE: the safe direction, loud, and unable to
    #   hide a boilerplate string.
    #
    #   INVALID BRE - `[unclosed`, a trailing `\`: grep does not over-match, it
    #   refuses to compile the pattern. It exits 2 and prints nothing, which is
    #   indistinguishable from "no match" unless the exit status is read. A
    #   false NEGATIVE: the UNSAFE direction, and silent - the term is counted
    #   in `deny-list terms: N` while nothing is scanned with it.
    #
    # Hence both defences: the deny-list preflight above compiles every term
    # before the scan starts, and the exit status below is read on every file.
    # None of the nine terms above contains a metacharacter; whoever adds a
    # tenth must keep that true or escape it - and if they do not, the run
    # aborts instead of reporting `findings: 0`. (The count read "seven" while
    # the list held eight; T-703 corrected it and added the ninth.)
    _out=$(grep -Iin -- "$_term" "$_file" 2>/dev/null)
    _grc=$?
    if [ "$_grc" -ge 2 ]; then
      grep_failed "$_grc" "$_term" "$_file"
      continue
    fi
    # Without this guard an empty result would be printed as one blank hit line
    # and counted as a finding.
    [ -n "$_out" ] || continue
    printf '%s\n' "$_out" | while IFS= read -r _hit; do
      printf '%s|%s|%s\n' "$_label" "$_term" "$_hit"
    done
  done
}

# Findings are collected in a file: the `while` loops above run in subshells, so
# a counter incremented inside them would be lost. I-007: a count that silently
# resets to 0 is worse than no count at all.
HITS_FILE=$(mktemp -t no-boilerplate.XXXXXX) || exit 1
WORK_DIR=$(mktemp -d -t no-boilerplate.XXXXXX) || exit 1
GREP_ERR_FILE=$(mktemp -t no-boilerplate-grep.XXXXXX) || exit 1
# T-0632. The two RECEIPT files are written from inside the scanning
# subshells, for the same reason HITS_FILE is: a counter incremented there
# would be lost, and "how far did this pass get" is precisely the question a
# lost counter cannot answer. SKIP_FILE is the ledger that names every
# skipped path, so no skip count is ever a bare number.
PASS1_FILE=$(mktemp -t no-boilerplate-p1.XXXXXX) || exit 1
PASS2_FILE=$(mktemp -t no-boilerplate-p2.XXXXXX) || exit 1
SKIP_FILE=$(mktemp -t no-boilerplate-skip.XXXXXX) || exit 1
trap 'rm -rf -- "$HITS_FILE" "$WORK_DIR" "$GREP_ERR_FILE" "$PASS1_FILE" "$PASS2_FILE" "$SKIP_FILE"' EXIT INT TERM

# --------------------------------------------- pass 1: packaged tree at HEAD --
printf '\n-- pass 1: packaged tree at HEAD (what Drupal.org ships) ------------------------------------------------\n'
printf '%s\n' "$SCOPE_FILES" | while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  _tmp="$WORK_DIR/head_$(printf '%s' "$f" | tr '/' '_')"
  if git show "HEAD:$f" > "$_tmp" 2>/dev/null; then
    scan_one "HEAD:$f" "$_tmp"
  else
    # T-0632: the redirect above CREATES $_tmp before git runs, so a failed
    # `git show` leaves an EMPTY file behind - which the recount's text/binary
    # probe answers rc 1 for, i.e. it would be filed as a binary blob. Remove
    # it, so an unreadable path lands in the same NOT EXTRACTED bucket as one
    # the loop never reached. Both mean the same thing: not searched.
    rm -f -- "$_tmp"
    printf 'UNREADABLE|-|%s\n' "$f"
  fi
  # The receipt is written LAST, so it records a path this pass FINISHED
  # rather than one it started.
  printf '%s\n' "$f" >> "$PASS1_FILE"
done >> "$HITS_FILE"

# ------------------------------------- pass 2: working copies of those paths --
printf -- '-- pass 2: working copies of the same paths (catch it before the commit) ---------------------------------\n'
printf '%s\n' "$SCOPE_FILES" | while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  if [ -f "$f" ]; then
    scan_one "WORKTREE:$f" "$f"
  else
    printf 'DELETED|-|%s\n' "$f"
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$f" >> "$PASS2_FILE"
done >> "$HITS_FILE"

# Recount in the parent shell: the subshell counters above did not survive.
#
# T-0632 splits this PER PASS. One merged BINARY counter is what let 364
# never-extracted paths hide inside a number whose honest value was 3+3: with
# the passes separated, each one has to add up to the declared scope on its
# own, and an inflated bucket has nowhere to sit. The fed-by-heredoc `while`
# replaces `for f in $(...)`: a heredoc is a REDIRECT, not a pipe, so the loop
# runs in THIS shell and the counters survive - which is why the `for` was
# there - while `IFS= read -r` also stops a path containing whitespace from
# being split into two paths that are each counted and neither scanned.
HEAD_SCANNED=0
HEAD_BINARY=0
HEAD_NOT_EXTRACTED=0
WT_SCANNED=0
WT_BINARY=0
WT_ABSENT=0
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  _tmp="$WORK_DIR/head_$(printf '%s' "$f" | tr '/' '_')"
  # Written as separate statements rather than `[ -f ] && grep -Iq`, because the
  # `&&` form throws grep's exit status away and cannot tell rc 1 (binary) from
  # rc >= 2 (grep failed) - which is how an unscannable file used to be counted
  # as a scanned-and-clean one.
  if [ -f "$_tmp" ]; then
    grep -Iq . "$_tmp" 2>/dev/null
    _rc=$?
    [ "$_rc" -ge 2 ] && grep_failed "$_rc" "<non-empty-line test: the text/binary probe>" "$_tmp"
    if [ "$_rc" -eq 0 ]; then
      HEAD_SCANNED=$((HEAD_SCANNED + 1))
    else
      HEAD_BINARY=$((HEAD_BINARY + 1))
      printf 'pass 1|binary|%s\n' "$f" >> "$SKIP_FILE"
    fi
  else
    # THE LINE THIS WHOLE CHANGE IS ABOUT. It used to read
    # `BINARY=$((BINARY + 1))`. A packaged path with no HEAD copy was never
    # searched; it is not a binary blob, and it is not clean.
    HEAD_NOT_EXTRACTED=$((HEAD_NOT_EXTRACTED + 1))
    printf 'pass 1|NOT EXTRACTED|%s\n' "$f" >> "$SKIP_FILE"
  fi
  if [ -f "$f" ]; then
    grep -Iq . "$f" 2>/dev/null
    _rc=$?
    [ "$_rc" -ge 2 ] && grep_failed "$_rc" "<non-empty-line test: the text/binary probe>" "$f"
    if [ "$_rc" -eq 0 ]; then
      WT_SCANNED=$((WT_SCANNED + 1))
    else
      WT_BINARY=$((WT_BINARY + 1))
      printf 'pass 2|binary|%s\n' "$f" >> "$SKIP_FILE"
    fi
  else
    WT_ABSENT=$((WT_ABSENT + 1))
    printf 'pass 2|absent|%s\n' "$f" >> "$SKIP_FILE"
  fi
done <<RECOUNT
$SCOPE_FILES
RECOUNT

# The totals the summary has always printed, now DERIVED from the per-pass
# counters rather than accumulated into one bucket.
SCANNED=$((HEAD_SCANNED + WT_SCANNED))
BINARY=$((HEAD_BINARY + WT_BINARY))
MISSING=$WT_ABSENT

# Before the findings block and the counts: any grep that failed - in this shell
# or in either scanning subshell - stops the run here, loudly.
assert_grep_ok

# ------------------------------------------ reconciliation (T-0632) ---------
# `assert_grep_ok` above answers "did a grep fail?". This answers the other
# way a scan does not happen: "did a pass stop?". They are siblings, and the
# second had no guard at all - see the block at the top of this file.
#
# Every FATAL below gets its OWN sentence. "The reconciliation failed" would
# leave a reader to work out which of six things went wrong, and the whole
# complaint against the old behaviour is that it made a reader work out what a
# number meant.
RECON_BAD=0
recon_fail() { # <sentence>
  printf '  %sFATAL:%s %s\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$1"
  RECON_BAD=$((RECON_BAD + 1))
}

# `wc -l` and not `grep -c`: all three feed `[` tests below (house rule,
# I-031). A missing receipt file is impossible here - mktemp created all three
# or the script exited - but `wc -l <` on one would print nothing, and
# `${:-0}` at each use site turns that blank into the FAIL direction.
PASS1_DONE=$(wc -l < "$PASS1_FILE" | tr -d '[:space:]')
PASS2_DONE=$(wc -l < "$PASS2_FILE" | tr -d '[:space:]')
SKIPS_NAMED=$(wc -l < "$SKIP_FILE" | tr -d '[:space:]')
SKIPS_COUNTED=$((HEAD_BINARY + HEAD_NOT_EXTRACTED + WT_BINARY + WT_ABSENT))
ACCOUNTED=$((SCANNED + SKIPS_COUNTED))
EXPECTED=$((FILE_COUNT * 2))

printf '\n-- reconciliation: every packaged path accounted for, in both passes -------------------------------------\n'
printf '  scope declared by `git archive HEAD`   %d file(s)\n' "$FILE_COUNT"
printf '  pass 1  HEAD copies      finished %d   text %d   binary %d   NOT EXTRACTED %d\n' \
  "${PASS1_DONE:-0}" "$HEAD_SCANNED" "$HEAD_BINARY" "$HEAD_NOT_EXTRACTED"
printf '  pass 2  working copies   finished %d   text %d   binary %d   absent %d\n' \
  "${PASS2_DONE:-0}" "$WT_SCANNED" "$WT_BINARY" "$WT_ABSENT"

if [ "${PASS1_DONE:-0}" -ne "$FILE_COUNT" ]; then
  recon_fail "$(printf 'pass 1 finished %s of %s packaged path(s) - it ended early, and the rest were never searched.' \
    "${PASS1_DONE:-0}" "$FILE_COUNT")"
fi
if [ "${PASS2_DONE:-0}" -ne "$FILE_COUNT" ]; then
  recon_fail "$(printf 'pass 2 finished %s of %s packaged path(s) - it ended early, and the rest were never searched.' \
    "${PASS2_DONE:-0}" "$FILE_COUNT")"
fi
if [ "$HEAD_NOT_EXTRACTED" -gt 0 ]; then
  recon_fail "$(printf '%s packaged path(s) have no HEAD copy in the work dir - not scanned, and NOT binary blobs.' \
    "$HEAD_NOT_EXTRACTED")"
fi
if [ "$((HEAD_SCANNED + HEAD_BINARY + HEAD_NOT_EXTRACTED))" -ne "$FILE_COUNT" ]; then
  recon_fail "$(printf 'pass 1 accounts for %s path(s), scope declares %s.' \
    "$((HEAD_SCANNED + HEAD_BINARY + HEAD_NOT_EXTRACTED))" "$FILE_COUNT")"
fi
if [ "$((WT_SCANNED + WT_BINARY + WT_ABSENT))" -ne "$FILE_COUNT" ]; then
  recon_fail "$(printf 'pass 2 accounts for %s path(s), scope declares %s.' \
    "$((WT_SCANNED + WT_BINARY + WT_ABSENT))" "$FILE_COUNT")"
fi
if [ "$ACCOUNTED" -ne "$EXPECTED" ]; then
  recon_fail "$(printf 'accounted for %s path-scan(s), the declared scope is %s (%s paths x 2 passes).' \
    "$ACCOUNTED" "$EXPECTED" "$FILE_COUNT")"
fi
# I-028 on the ledger itself: an emptied SKIP_FILE under a healthy skip count
# would read as "nothing was skipped", which is the same lie one level down.
if [ "${SKIPS_NAMED:-0}" -ne "$SKIPS_COUNTED" ]; then
  recon_fail "$(printf '%s skip(s) counted but %s named - a skip nobody can attribute to a path is refused.' \
    "$SKIPS_COUNTED" "${SKIPS_NAMED:-0}")"
fi

printf '  accounted %d of %d path-scan(s)   skips named %d of %d counted\n' \
  "$ACCOUNTED" "$EXPECTED" "${SKIPS_NAMED:-0}" "$SKIPS_COUNTED"
if [ "$SKIPS_COUNTED" -gt 0 ]; then
  while IFS='|' read -r _sp _sr _skip_path; do
    [ -n "$_sp" ] || continue
    printf '      %s  %-13s %s\n' "$_sp" "$_sr" "$_skip_path"
  done < "$SKIP_FILE"
fi

if [ "$RECON_BAD" -gt 0 ]; then
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s the scan did not cover the scope it declared (%s reconciliation failure(s)).\n' \
    "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$RECON_BAD"
  printf 'A scan that did not happen is NOT a scan that found nothing. This run has\n'
  printf 'NO result, which is not a clean result. (T-0632, and I-027 for the sibling case)\n'
  exit 1
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------- I-007 self-guard ----
# Every count above is built from PACKAGED_FILES, which is filtered out of the
# archive listing by a bare `grep -v '/$'` (line 158) that never checks its own
# exit status. On rc >= 2 that filter prints nothing, PACKAGED_FILES is empty,
# both scanning passes and the recount above iterate zero times, and SCANNED
# reaches 0 without a single grep_failed() ever firing - assert_grep_ok has
# nothing to report, because nothing was ever told to report. `no-boilerplate`
# was the only one of the seven invariants without this guard, even though the
# CONTRACT at the top of this file already promises it. Match the other six:
# SCANNED == 0 is a FAILURE, never a clean run over nothing (I-007).
#
# T-321(b): the input is defaulted. Zero means FAIL here, which is the SAFE
# direction of I-031 - a blank would make `[` exit 2 and the guard would be
# stepped over rather than tripped. Do not copy this `:-0` to a guard where
# zero means PASS.
if [ "${SCANNED:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s scanned 0 files - the scope collapsed to nothing.\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF"
  printf 'A scan of nothing is not a clean result (I-007).\n'
  exit 1
fi

# The other half of the same degenerate scope (T-320a): this invariant's scope
# is files x terms, and TERM_COUNT - fixed at parse time from the DENY_TERMS
# heredoc above, never touched by the scan itself - can collapse to zero just
# as SCANNED can. A run with no terms would still scan every file, find
# nothing to match anything against, and print `deny-list terms: 0 - findings:
# 0`, exit 0 - a clean-looking result for a scan that had nothing to look for.
# TERM_COUNT == 0 is a FAILURE for the same reason SCANNED == 0 is (I-007), and
# T-321(b) defaults its input for the same reason as the guard above: zero means
# FAIL here, so `:-0` trips the guard instead of letting a blank step over it.
if [ "${TERM_COUNT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s deny-list terms: 0 - the scope collapsed to nothing.\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF"
  printf 'A scan with no deny-list terms is not a clean result (I-007).\n'
  exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- findings ---
# T-316 / I-027, second bite: `grep -cve '^$' "$HITS_FILE" || FINDINGS=0` fixed
# the concatenation bug but kept the `||` itself, which still turns a grep that
# FAILED (rc >= 2: this grep is not one of the call sites assert_grep_ok covers)
# into "zero findings" - indistinguishable from a grep that legitimately matched
# nothing. `wc -l` has no third exit state to swallow: it counts the newlines in
# HITS_FILE or it does not run, full stop. This is count_findings() from
# no-patches / no-unstable-deps / sbom-check, applied here to HITS_FILE.
FINDINGS=$(wc -l < "$HITS_FILE" | tr -d '[:space:]')

printf '\n-- findings ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n'
if [ "$FINDINGS" -eq 0 ]; then
  printf '  %snone%s\n' "$C_OK" "$C_OFF"
else
  while IFS='|' read -r _label _term _hit; do
    [ -z "$_label" ] && continue
    printf '  %sBOILERPLATE%s  %s  [%s]\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$_label" "$_term"
    printf '      | %s\n' "$_hit"
  done < "$HITS_FILE"
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ counts ---
printf '\n=========================================================================================================\n'
printf 'scope: packaged tree (%d entries) + their working copies - scanned: %d - deny-list terms: %d - findings: %d\n' \
  "$ENTRIES" "$SCANNED" "$TERM_COUNT" "$FINDINGS"
# T-0632. The gate asserts this figure is positive, which is the I-028 guard
# on the reconciliation having happened AT ALL: delete the block above and
# this line goes with it, the runner's extractor returns nothing, and G7 fails
# naming it - instead of the invariant quietly going back to being silent at
# partial. Its VALUE is checked by the reconciliation itself, above, which is
# where the arithmetic is; the runner checks that there is a value.
printf 'accounted: %d of %d packaged path-scan(s) - %d path(s) x 2 passes - skips named: %d\n' \
  "$ACCOUNTED" "$EXPECTED" "$FILE_COUNT" "${SKIPS_NAMED:-0}"
if [ "$BINARY" -gt 0 ] || [ "$MISSING" -gt 0 ]; then
  printf '%s(skipped: %d binary blob(s); %d packaged path(s) absent from the working tree)%s\n' \
    "$C_DIM" "$BINARY" "$MISSING" "$C_OFF"
fi
printf '=========================================================================================================\n'

[ "$FINDINGS" -eq 0 ] && exit 0
exit 1
